WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:04.520
- I know it's hard to pick favorites,

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:07.061
but what is your favorite matrix?

00:00:07.061 --> 00:00:08.390
- (laughing) What's my favorite matrix?

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.240
Okay, so my favorite matrix
is square, I admit it.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.500
It's a square bunch of numbers

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.230
and it has twos running
down the main diagonal.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.060
And, on the next diagonal,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.400
so think of top left to bottom right,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.940
twos down the middle of the matrix,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:31.160
and minus ones just above those twos,

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.020
and minus ones just below those twos.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.180
And, otherwise, all
zeros, so mostly zeros.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.900
Just three non-zero diagonals coming down.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.370
- What is interesting about it?

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:46.200
- Well, all the different
ways it comes up.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.250
You see it in engineering.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:53.120
You see it as an ALEKS in
calculus to second derivative.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.620
So calculus learns about
taking the derivative,

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.663
figuring out how fast
something's changing,

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.740
but second derivative,
now that's also important.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.770
That's how fast the change is changing,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:12.770
how fast the graph is bending,
how fast it's curving.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.650
And, Einstein showed that

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:20.593
that's fundamental to understand space.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.300
So second derivatives should have

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.380
a bigger place in calculus.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:31.180
Second, my matrices, which are like

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.030
the linear algebra version,
of second derivatives,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:39.020
are neat in, linear algebra.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.857
Yeah, just everything comes
out right with those guys!

00:01:41.857 --> 00:01:43.080
(laughter)

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:43.913
- Beautiful!

